    "updated_at": "2015-09-02T08:34:49Z",
    "body": "This PR scares me as-is, since the new text is right only if the implementation does not implement any non-Encoding Standard encodings, but the spec doesn't prohibit non-Encoding Standard encodings. Specifically, the first paragraph of \"12.2.2.3 Character encodings\" says \"User agents must support the encodings defined in the WHATWG Encoding standard. User agents should not support other encodings.\"\n\nI suggest the following further changes:\n1. s/should/must/ in the first paragraph of \"12.2.2.3 Character encodings\" so that it says \"User agents must support the encodings defined in the WHATWG Encoding standard. User agents _must_ not support other encodings.\"\n2. Delete paragraphs 2 and 3 of that section. (The ones that talk about CESU-8, UTF-7, BOCU-1, SCSU, EBCDIC and UTF-32.)\n3. Replace the note in that section with \"Note: The above prohibits supporting, for example, CESU-8, UTF-7, BOCU-1, SCSU, EBCDIC and UTF-32. This specification does not make any attempt to support prohibited in its algorithms; support and use of prohibited encodings would thus lead to unexpected behaviour.\"\n4. After the (new) definition of \"ASCII-compatible encoding\", add \"Note: Since support for encodings that are not defined in the WHATWG Encoding standard is prohibited, UTF-16 encodings are the only encodings that this specification needs to treat as not being ASCII-compatible encodings.\"\n5. In section \"4.2.5.5 Specifying the document's character encoding\" remove the paragraph starting \"Encodings in which a series of bytes\" and the paragraph starting with \"Authors should not use UTF-32\".\n6. In their place add: \"Authors must not use encodings that are not defined in the WHATWG Encoding standard. Note: Some encodings that are not defined in the WHATWG Encoding standard use bytes in the range 0x20 to 0x7E to encode characters other than the corresponding characters in the range U+0020 to U+007E and represent a potential security vulnerability: A user agent might end up interpreting supposedly benign plain text content as HTML tags and JavaScript.\"\n\nAs a bonus, you could add \"Additionally, authors must not use ISO-2022-JP.\", but I have not seen a way for ISO-2022-JP to cause XSS the way ISO-2022-KR could.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
